{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BnhEG93VB3Z9j7ujeXv2E1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Implementation translation guide (Phase 4)\n2\t\n3\tA focused reference for filling in `todo!()` bodies in\n4\t`rs/fq/src/`.  Companion to `xlate/test_translation_guide.md`\n5\t(which covers the test side); they share the API surface\n6\toverview and naming conventions, but this guide focuses on\n7\t*body* translation.\n8\t\n9\t**Read this first.**\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## Operating mandate\n14\t\n15\t* **Translate every C body faithfully.**  Walk `picoquic/<src>.c`\n16\t  for each function whose Rust counterpart is `todo!()` and write\n17\t  the equivalent Rust body.  Don't sidestep with a simpler-looking\n18\t  algorithm — the C body is the spec.\n19\t* **Tests are the gate.**  After each meaningful chunk, run\n20\t  `cd rs/fq && cargo test` and watch the panic count drop.  A\n21\t  function is \"done\" when the tests that reach it stop panicking.\n22\t* **Idiomatic Rust over C-mirroring.**  Phase 1 already shaped\n23\t  signatures.  Phase 4 is where the shape pays off — use Rust\n24\t  idioms in the body even when they don't match the C control\n25\t  flow.\n26\t* **No `unsafe`.**  No edits outside `rs/fq/src/`.\n27\t\n28\t---\n29\t\n30\t## C body patterns → Rust idioms\n31\t\n32\t### Memory and ownership\n33\t\n34\t| C pattern | Rust translation |\n35\t|---|---|\n36\t| `T* p = malloc(sizeof(T)); ...; free(p);` | `let p = Box::new(T { ... });` (auto `Drop`) |\n37\t| `T arr[N]` on stack, returns by ptr | `[T; N]` or `&[T; N]`, never `*const T` |\n38\t| `(uint8_t* buf, size_t len)` aliasing call | `&[u8]` (read) / `&mut [u8]` (write); slice carries len |\n39\t| Linked list: `T* head; T* cur->next` | `Vec<T>` or `VecDeque<T>` if FIFO; iterate with `.iter()` |\n40\t| Intrusive node: `struct T { …; next *T; }` | Token-based: parent owns `Vec<T>`, callers hold an [`Arena`]/[`SplayTree`]/[`HashTable`] token |\n41\t| `void* opaque_handle` | `Box<dyn Trait>` (one trait per shape) |\n42\t| `static T global` | `static T` if `T: Sync` else `pub fn name() -> T` accessor |\n43\t\n44\t### Control flow\n45\t\n46\t| C pattern | Rust translation |\n47\t|---|---|\n48\t| `if (ret == 0) { ... } else { ret = -1; }` | `if x { … }`; propagate fallibility with `?` |\n49\t| `goto cleanup; cleanup: free(p); return ret;` | Rust `Drop` runs automatically; `?` for early-return |\n50\t| `int ret = 0; ... ret \\|= f(...); ... return ret;` | Compose `Result` with `?`; never use bitwise-OR for status |\n51\t| `for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) { ... }` | `for i in 0..n { ... }` or `.iter().enumerate()` |\n52\t| `while (cur != NULL) { ... cur = cur->next; }` | `while let Some(node) = ...` or `.iter()` |\n53\t| `switch (e) { case X: ...; case Y: ...; default: ...; }` | `match e { X => ..., Y => ..., _ => ... }`, exhaustive |\n54\t| `assert(cond)` | `assert!(cond)` or `debug_assert!(cond)` |\n55\t\n56\t### Errors and returns\n57\t\n58\t| C pattern | Rust translation |\n59\t|---|---|\n60\t| `int` 0/-1 status | `Result<(), Error>`; map `0 → Ok(())`, `-1 → Err(Error::…)` |\n61\t| `int` 0/PICOQUIC_ERROR_X | `Result<T, Error>`; map error codes to specific `Error::…` variants |\n62\t| Out-parameter `T* result` | `-> Result<T, Error>` returning the value by-move |\n63\t| `size_t` returning bytes-written, with `SIZE_MAX` on failure | `Result<usize, Error>`, never `usize::MAX` as a sentinel |\n64\t| `bool` returning 0/1 | `bool` |\n65\t\n66\t### Strings and bytes\n67\t\n68\t| C pattern | Rust translation |\n69\t|---|---|\n70\t| `char const*` (read-only string) | `&str` |\n71\t| `char*` (owned) | `String` |\n72\t| `(char* str, size_t cap)` (caller-allocated) | `&mut [u8]` or `&mut String`; document encoding |\n73\t| `memcpy(dst, src, n)` | `dst.copy_from_slice(src)` (if same len) |\n74\t| `memset(buf, c, n)` | `buf.fill(c)` |\n75\t| `memcmp(a, b, n)` | `a == b` (slice-equality) or `a.cmp(b)` |\n76\t| `picoquic_constant_time_memcmp` | [`crate::utils::constant_time_memcmp`] |\n77\t\n78\t### Time\n79\t\n80\t* `uint64_t current_time` (microseconds) → [`crate::Instant`] (`fugit::Instant<u64, 1, 1_000_000>`).\n81\t* Duration / RTT / interval → [`crate::Duration`].\n82\t* Construct with `Instant::from_ticks(usec)` / `Duration::from_ticks(usec)`.\n83\t* Subtract instants: `now - earlier` returns `Duration`.\n84\t\n85\t### Network\n86\t\n87\t* `struct sockaddr*` → `&core::net::SocketAddr`.\n88\t* IP address stored as bytes → `core::net::IpAddr`.\n89\t* Port byte-order: keep host-order in Rust; do `.to_be_bytes()` /\n90\t  `.from_be_bytes` only at the wire boundary.\n91\t\n92\t### Logging / formatting\n93\t\n94\t* `printf` / `fprintf` / `picoquic_log_xxx` → `log::{trace, debug,\n95\t  info, warn, error}` macros where appropriate; or write into a\n96\t  `&mut impl core::fmt::Write` buffer for the existing logger\n97\t  callbacks.\n98\t* `va_list` / variadic → `core::fmt::Arguments<'_>`; the call site\n99\t  uses `format_args!`.\n100\t* `snprintf` / `sprintf` → `format!` / `write!` / `core::fmt::Write`.\n101\t* Hex / binary formatting: use `{:02x}`, `{:08b}`, etc.\n102\t\n103\t### Crypto\n104\t\n105\t* AES / ChaCha20: use the Rust Crypto trait crates (`aead`,\n106\t  `cipher`, `digest`) and concrete impls (`aes`, `chacha20`).\n107\t* Header protection lives in [`crate::header_protection`]; AEAD\n108\t  packet-key plumbing in [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] (impls in\n109\t  [`crate::sys::picotls`] / [`crate::sys::openssl`] backends).\n110\t* HKDF with SHA-256 for QUIC initial secrets (RFC 9001 fixes\n111\t  this); use the `hkdf` crate.\n112\t\n113\t---\n114\t\n115\t## The \"tests are the gate\" workflow\n116\t\n117\tFor each translated function:\n118\t\n119\t1. **Find the tests that exercise it.**  `grep -lE \"<fn name>\" rs/fq/src/tests/`\n120\t   often points right at them.  The Phase 3 known-answer tests\n121\t   (intformat, varint, siphash, splay, hashtest, code_version)\n122\t   exercise data-structure leaves first.\n123\t2. **Translate the body.**  Use Edit; preserve the existing\n124\t   signature and doc comment.  Don't rename / reshape.\n125\t3. **Run `cargo test <test_name>`.**  If it still panics, the\n126\t   panic backtrace tells you which other `todo!()` is in the\n127\t   way.  Translate that next, then rerun.\n128\t4. **Repeat until the test passes.**  Then move to the next test.\n129\t\n130\tBottom-up order is the natural one.  In the Phase 0 call graph,\n131\tfunction-level \"max height\" gives the depth.  Leaves first means\n132\tdata-structure tests light up first; connection-lifecycle tests\n133\tlast.\n134\t\n135\t---\n136\t\n137\t## When to add new types\n138\t\n139\t* The C type catalog is in [`crate::internal`] (private structs\n140\t  internal to the library) and [`crate::lib`] (public types\n141\t  re-exported at the crate root).  These are stable; don't add\n142\t  fields unless absolutely necessary.\n143\t* If the C body needs a `static` table (e.g. cipher-suite\n144\t  registries, frame-name tables, per-version parameter blocks),\n145\t  put it in the same module as the function that consumes it,\n146\t  as a `pub(crate) const` or `static`.\n147\t* Helpers that are only used by one function: nest with `fn`\n148\t  inside that function or as a private `fn` immediately above.\n149\t  Keep the public surface lean.\n150\t\n151\t---\n152\t\n153\t## Conservative refactors allowed\n154\t\n155\tThe Phase 4 prompt forbids \"rename / reshape\" of existing\n156\titems.  But these refactors are explicitly allowed:\n157\t\n158\t* **Splitting a long fn into helpers** when the C body has a\n159\t  natural segmentation (e.g. parse → validate → record).  Keep\n160\t  the public surface — the helpers are private.\n161\t* **Replacing a manual loop with an iterator chain** when the\n162\t  semantics are exactly preserved.\n163\t* **Replacing a manual `for` with `.copy_from_slice`,\n164\t  `.iter().sum()`, `.windows(N)`, etc.**\n165\t* **Changing local variable types** (e.g. `usize` → `u32` if the\n166\t  range fits) when the body's invariants justify it.\n167\t\n168\tIf a refactor crosses a public-API boundary (changing a\n169\tparameter type, adding a return value, splitting one fn into\n170\ttwo public ones), surface it on stdout and stop — that's a\n171\thuman design call.\n172\t\n173\t---\n174\t\n175\t## Module map (where to translate what)\n176\t\n177\t| Rust module | C source | Notes |\n178\t|---|---|---|\n179\t| `crate::siphash` | `picoquic/picohash.c` (siphash bit) | Known-answer tested.  Pure leaf. |\n180\t| `crate::splay` | `picoquic/picosplay.c` | Token-based splay tree; tests cover insert/find/delete/iter. |\n181\t| `crate::hash` | `picoquic/picohash.c` | Hash table over arena tokens. |\n182\t| `crate::arena` | (Phase 2 design — no C counterpart) | Slotmap. |\n183\t| `crate::bytestream` | `picoquic/bytestream.c` | Cursor over `&mut [u8]` plus owned-buffer helper. |\n184\t| `crate::utils` | `picoquic/picoquic_utils.c` | uint8_to_str, constant_time_memcmp, addr helpers. |\n185\t| `crate::errors` | `picoquic/picoquic_internal.h` consts | `name()` lookup tables for the two enum sets. |\n186\t| `crate::frames::FrameType::name` | `picoquic/frames.c::picoquic_frame_name` | Lookup table. |\n187\t| `crate::tp::TransportParameter::name` | `picoquic/transport.c::picoquic_tp_name` | Lookup table. |\n188\t| `crate::internal::{parse,format}_*` | `picoquic/picoquic_utils.c` | One-line bodies; mostly already translated. |\n189\t| `crate::internal::varint_*` | `picoquic/frames.c` | RFC 9000 §16. |\n190\t| `crate::internal::get_packet_number64` | `picoquic/packet.c` | RFC 9000 §A.3 reconstruction. |\n191\t| `crate::header_protection` | `picoquic/protoop_aead.c` (header bits) | RFC 9001 §5.4. |\n192\t| `crate::sys::picotls` / `::openssl` | picotls / openssl bindings | Phase 4 wires real crypto. |\n193\t| `crate::tls_api::*` | `picoquic/tls_api.c` | Master TLS context, retry tokens, app-secret rotation. |\n194\t| `crate::lb::*` | `picoquic/cid_for_lb.c` | LB CID encryption. |\n195\t| `crate::config::*` | `picoquic/quic_config.c` (sample-app uses) | Command-line option parsing. |\n196\t| `crate::binlog`, `::logger`, `::qlog`, `::textlog` | `loglib/*` | (out of v1 scope per plan; if any todo!() left, leave them) |\n197\t| `crate::packet_loop` | `picoquic/sockloop.c` | Network event loop. |\n198\t| `crate::socks*` | `picoquic/picosocks.c` | UDP socket abstraction. |\n199\t| `crate::internal::Quic` / `Connection` / `Path` impls | `picoquic/quicctx.c`, `cnx_*.c`, `paths.c`, … | The bulk: ~200 fns each on Quic / Connection. |\n200\t\n201\tRecommended order (bottom-up): siphash, splay, hash, arena (no\n202\ttodo!()s left there?), bytestream, utils, errors, frames, tp,\n203\tvarint helpers, get_packet_number64, header_protection, then up\n204\tto lb, config, tls_api, then Connection / Path / Quic core, then\n205\tsys backends, then network event loop / socks.\n206\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6e30a456-15c0-4e3c-af27-9f4dab375915","uuid":"eeddbaff-4a68-4e0b-8235-cf0eb480c15b","timestamp":"2026-05-05T21:18:37.688Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/xlate/impl_translation_guide.md","content":"# Implementation translation guide (Phase 4)\n\nA focused reference for filling in `todo!()` bodies in\n`rs/fq/src/`.  Companion to `xlate/test_translation_guide.md`\n(which covers the test side); they share the API surface\noverview and naming conventions, but this guide focuses on\n*body* translation.\n\n**Read this first.**\n\n---\n\n## Operating mandate\n\n* **Translate every C body faithfully.**  Walk `picoquic/<src>.c`\n  for each function whose Rust counterpart is `todo!()` and write\n  the equivalent Rust body.  Don't sidestep with a simpler-looking\n  algorithm — the C body is the spec.\n* **Tests are the gate.**  After each meaningful chunk, run\n  `cd rs/fq && cargo test` and watch the panic count drop.  A\n  function is \"done\" when the tests that reach it stop panicking.\n* **Idiomatic Rust over C-mirroring.**  Phase 1 already shaped\n  signatures.  Phase 4 is where the shape pays off — use Rust\n  idioms in the body even when they don't match the C control\n  flow.\n* **No `unsafe`.**  No edits outside `rs/fq/src/`.\n\n---\n\n## C body patterns → Rust idioms\n\n### Memory and ownership\n\n| C pattern | Rust translation |\n|---|---|\n| `T* p = malloc(sizeof(T)); ...; free(p);` | `let p = Box::new(T { ... });` (auto `Drop`) |\n| `T arr[N]` on stack, returns by ptr | `[T; N]` or `&[T; N]`, never `*const T` |\n| `(uint8_t* buf, size_t len)` aliasing call | `&[u8]` (read) / `&mut [u8]` (write); slice carries len |\n| Linked list: `T* head; T* cur->next` | `Vec<T>` or `VecDeque<T>` if FIFO; iterate with `.iter()` |\n| Intrusive node: `struct T { …; next *T; }` | Token-based: parent owns `Vec<T>`, callers hold an [`Arena`]/[`SplayTree`]/[`HashTable`] token |\n| `void* opaque_handle` | `Box<dyn Trait>` (one trait per shape) |\n| `static T global` | `static T` if `T: Sync` else `pub fn name() -> T` accessor |\n\n### Control flow\n\n| C pattern | Rust translation |\n|---|---|\n| `if (ret == 0) { ... } else { ret = -1; }` | `if x { … }`; propagate fallibility with `?` |\n| `goto cleanup; cleanup: free(p); return ret;` | Rust `Drop` runs automatically; `?` for early-return |\n| `int ret = 0; ... ret \\|= f(...); ... return ret;` | Compose `Result` with `?`; never use bitwise-OR for status |\n| `for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) { ... }` | `for i in 0..n { ... }` or `.iter().enumerate()` |\n| `while (cur != NULL) { ... cur = cur->next; }` | `while let Some(node) = ...` or `.iter()` |\n| `switch (e) { case X: ...; case Y: ...; default: ...; }` | `match e { X => ..., Y => ..., _ => ... }`, exhaustive |\n| `assert(cond)` | `assert!(cond)` or `debug_assert!(cond)` |\n\n### Errors and returns\n\n| C pattern | Rust translation |\n|---|---|\n| `int` 0/-1 status | `Result<(), Error>`; map `0 → Ok(())`, `-1 → Err(Error::…)` |\n| `int` 0/PICOQUIC_ERROR_X | `Result<T, Error>`; map error codes to specific `Error::…` variants |\n| Out-parameter `T* result` | `-> Result<T, Error>` returning the value by-move |\n| `size_t` returning bytes-written, with `SIZE_MAX` on failure | `Result<usize, Error>`, never `usize::MAX` as a sentinel |\n| `bool` returning 0/1 | `bool` |\n\n### Strings and bytes\n\n| C pattern | Rust translation |\n|---|---|\n| `char const*` (read-only string) | `&str` |\n| `char*` (owned) | `String` |\n| `(char* str, size_t cap)` (caller-allocated) | `&mut [u8]` or `&mut String`; document encoding |\n| `memcpy(dst, src, n)` | `dst.copy_from_slice(src)` (if same len) |\n| `memset(buf, c, n)` | `buf.fill(c)` |\n| `memcmp(a, b, n)` | `a == b` (slice-equality) or `a.cmp(b)` |\n| `picoquic_constant_time_memcmp` | [`crate::utils::constant_time_memcmp`] |\n\n### Time\n\n* `uint64_t current_time` (microseconds) → [`crate::Instant`] (`fugit::Instant<u64, 1, 1_000_000>`).\n* Duration / RTT / interval → [`crate::Duration`].\n* Construct with `Instant::from_ticks(usec)` / `Duration::from_ticks(usec)`.\n* Subtract instants: `now - earlier` returns `Duration`.\n\n### Network\n\n* `struct sockaddr*` → `&core::net::SocketAddr`.\n* IP address stored as bytes → `core::net::IpAddr`.\n* Port byte-order: keep host-order in Rust; do `.to_be_bytes()` /\n  `.from_be_bytes` only at the wire boundary.\n\n### Logging / formatting\n\n* `printf` / `fprintf` / `picoquic_log_xxx` → `log::{trace, debug,\n  info, warn, error}` macros where appropriate; or write into a\n  `&mut impl core::fmt::Write` buffer for the existing logger\n  callbacks.\n* `va_list` / variadic → `core::fmt::Arguments<'_>`; the call site\n  uses `format_args!`.\n* `snprintf` / `sprintf` → `format!` / `write!` / `core::fmt::Write`.\n* Hex / binary formatting: use `{:02x}`, `{:08b}`, etc.\n\n### Crypto\n\n* AES / ChaCha20: use the Rust Crypto trait crates (`aead`,\n  `cipher`, `digest`) and concrete impls (`aes`, `chacha20`).\n* Header protection lives in [`crate::header_protection`]; AEAD\n  packet-key plumbing in [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] (impls in\n  [`crate::sys::picotls`] / [`crate::sys::openssl`] backends).\n* HKDF with SHA-256 for QUIC initial secrets (RFC 9001 fixes\n  this); use the `hkdf` crate.\n\n---\n\n## The \"tests are the gate\" workflow\n\nFor each translated function:\n\n1. **Find the tests that exercise it.**  `grep -lE \"<fn name>\" rs/fq/src/tests/`\n   often points right at them.  The Phase 3 known-answer tests\n   (intformat, varint, siphash, splay, hashtest, code_version)\n   exercise data-structure leaves first.\n2. **Translate the body.**  Use Edit; preserve the existing\n   signature and doc comment.  Don't rename / reshape.\n3. **Run `cargo test <test_name>`.**  If it still panics, the\n   panic backtrace tells you which other `todo!()` is in the\n   way.  Translate that next, then rerun.\n4. **Repeat until the test passes.**  Then move to the next test.\n\nBottom-up order is the natural one.  In the Phase 0 call graph,\nfunction-level \"max height\" gives the depth.  Leaves first means\ndata-structure tests light up first; connection-lifecycle tests\nlast.\n\n---\n\n## When to add new types\n\n* The C type catalog is in [`crate::internal`] (private structs\n  internal to the library) and [`crate::lib`] (public types\n  re-exported at the crate root).  These are stable; don't add\n  fields unless absolutely necessary.\n* If the C body needs a `static` table (e.g. cipher-suite\n  registries, frame-name tables, per-version parameter blocks),\n  put it in the same module as the function that consumes it,\n  as a `pub(crate) const` or `static`.\n* Helpers that are only used by one function: nest with `fn`\n  inside that function or as a private `fn` immediately above.\n  Keep the public surface lean.\n\n---\n\n## Conservative refactors allowed\n\nThe Phase 4 prompt forbids \"rename / reshape\" of existing\nitems.  But these refactors are explicitly allowed:\n\n* **Splitting a long fn into helpers** when the C body has a\n  natural segmentation (e.g. parse → validate → record).  Keep\n  the public surface — the helpers are private.\n* **Replacing a manual loop with an iterator chain** when the\n  semantics are exactly preserved.\n* **Replacing a manual `for` with `.copy_from_slice`,\n  `.iter().sum()`, `.windows(N)`, etc.**\n* **Changing local variable types** (e.g. `usize` → `u32` if the\n  range fits) when the body's invariants justify it.\n\nIf a refactor crosses a public-API boundary (changing a\nparameter type, adding a return value, splitting one fn into\ntwo public ones), surface it on stdout and stop — that's a\nhuman design call.\n\n---\n\n## Module map (where to translate what)\n\n| Rust module | C source | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `crate::siphash` | `picoquic/picohash.c` (siphash bit) | Known-answer tested.  Pure leaf. |\n| `crate::splay` | `picoquic/picosplay.c` | Token-based splay tree; tests cover insert/find/delete/iter. |\n| `crate::hash` | `picoquic/picohash.c` | Hash table over arena tokens. |\n| `crate::arena` | (Phase 2 design — no C counterpart) | Slotmap. |\n| `crate::bytestream` | `picoquic/bytestream.c` | Cursor over `&mut [u8]` plus owned-buffer helper. |\n| `crate::utils` | `picoquic/picoquic_utils.c` | uint8_to_str, constant_time_memcmp, addr helpers. |\n| `crate::errors` | `picoquic/picoquic_internal.h` consts | `name()` lookup tables for the two enum sets. |\n| `crate::frames::FrameType::name` | `picoquic/frames.c::picoquic_frame_name` | Lookup table. |\n| `crate::tp::TransportParameter::name` | `picoquic/transport.c::picoquic_tp_name` | Lookup table. |\n| `crate::internal::{parse,format}_*` | `picoquic/picoquic_utils.c` | One-line bodies; mostly already translated. |\n| `crate::internal::varint_*` | `picoquic/frames.c` | RFC 9000 §16. |\n| `crate::internal::get_packet_number64` | `picoquic/packet.c` | RFC 9000 §A.3 reconstruction. |\n| `crate::header_protection` | `picoquic/protoop_aead.c` (header bits) | RFC 9001 §5.4. |\n| `crate::sys::picotls` / `::openssl` | picotls / openssl bindings | Phase 4 wires real crypto. |\n| `crate::tls_api::*` | `picoquic/tls_api.c` | Master TLS context, retry tokens, app-secret rotation. |\n| `crate::lb::*` | `picoquic/cid_for_lb.c` | LB CID encryption. |\n| `crate::config::*` | `picoquic/quic_config.c` (sample-app uses) | Command-line option parsing. |\n| `crate::binlog`, `::logger`, `::qlog`, `::textlog` | `loglib/*` | (out of v1 scope per plan; if any todo!() left, leave them) |\n| `crate::packet_loop` | `picoquic/sockloop.c` | Network event loop. |\n| `crate::socks*` | `picoquic/picosocks.c` | UDP socket abstraction. |\n| `crate::internal::Quic` / `Connection` / `Path` impls | `picoquic/quicctx.c`, `cnx_*.c`, `paths.c`, … | The bulk: ~200 fns each on Quic / Connection. |\n\nRecommended order (bottom-up): siphash, splay, hash, arena (no\ntodo!()s left there?), bytestream, utils, errors, frames, tp,\nvarint helpers, get_packet_number64, header_protection, then up\nto lb, config, tls_api, then Connection / Path / Quic core, then\nsys backends, then network event loop / socks.\n","numLines":206,"startLine":1,"totalLines":206}}}
