{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QmiDmfKbJouaFYo9aE7XXR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/quic.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! `quic.h` is the kitchen-sink public header for the\n4\t//! quic-core library: it defines the protocol error codes, the\n5\t//! transport-parameter identifiers, the application-facing enums, the\n6\t//! opaque QUIC / connection / path types, the public structs that\n7\t//! cross the API boundary (`TransportParameters`, `PathQuality`,\n8\t//! `PerAckState`, `CongestionAlgorithm`, …)\n9\t//! and the dozens of free functions that make up the application API.\n10\t//!\n11\t//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! Pointer-shape and translation policy notes that apply throughout\n14\t//! this module:\n15\t//!\n16\t//! * `Quic`, `Connection`, `Path` are\n17\t//!   opaque types defined in `internal.h`.  Phase 1 declares\n18\t//!   them as empty structs here so the public API can refer to them;\n19\t//!   the real layout lands when the internal header is translated.\n20\t//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields map to\n21\t//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`].  The C source uses tagged unions of\n22\t//!   `sockaddr_in` / `sockaddr_in6`, which `SocketAddr` models\n23\t//!   directly.  Output parameters of type `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n24\t//!   become a `SocketAddr` returned by value.\n25\t//! * Function-pointer typedefs become traits (one trait per typedef\n26\t//!   by default).  Trait grouping is used for the four-function\n27\t//!   `CongestionAlgorithm` vtable, where the C side\n28\t//!   always installs the four together.\n29\t//! * `void*` \"callback context\" arguments are folded into the trait\n30\t//!   implementor's state.  Per-stream / per-path application\n31\t//!   contexts that flow through the stack (e.g. `stream_ctx`,\n32\t//!   `app_stream_ctx`) become `Option<Box<dyn core::any::Any>>` —\n33\t//!   the application produces them, downcasts on retrieval.\n34\t//! * C `int` / `int64_t` return values that encode 0/-1 or 0/error\n35\t//!   status become `Result<T, Error>` — the crate-level `Error` enum\n36\t//!   doesn't exist yet, so `()` is a placeholder per the Phase 1\n37\t//!   contract.  `i32` / `i64` are kept where the C value is a real\n38\t//!   integer (e.g., wake delays in microseconds, interface index).\n39\t//! * Single-bit `unsigned int : 1` bitfields collapse to `bool`.\n40\t//! * `va_list` has no Rust counterpart; the `_v` log helpers are\n41\t//!   omitted in favour of one entry point that takes\n42\t//!   `core::fmt::Arguments<'_>`.\n43\t//! * `extern` global state (`congestion_control_algorithms`)\n44\t//!   becomes accessor functions that hand out a borrowed slice; the\n45\t//!   companion `_nb_` length variable disappears (the slice carries\n46\t//!   its length).\n47\t\n48\t// Many translated functions mirror C signatures with >7 parameters.\n49\t// Builder patterns or shape changes are out of scope for Phase 1.\n50\t#![allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n51\t\n52\tpub mod arena;\n53\tpub mod binlog;\n54\tpub mod bytestream;\n55\tpub mod cc_common;\n56\tpub mod config;\n57\tpub mod crypto;\n58\tpub mod errors;\n59\tpub mod frames;\n60\tpub mod hash;\n61\tpub mod header_protection;\n62\tpub mod internal;\n63\tpub mod lb;\n64\tpub mod logger;\n65\tpub mod packet_loop;\n66\tpub mod performance_log;\n67\tpub mod qlog;\n68\tpub mod siphash;\n69\tpub mod socks;\n70\tpub mod socks_socket2;\n71\tpub mod splay;\n72\tpub mod stream;\n73\tpub mod sys;\n74\t#[cfg(test)]\n75\tpub mod tests;\n76\tpub mod textlog;\n77\tpub mod tls;\n78\tpub mod tls_api;\n79\tpub mod tp;\n80\tpub mod utils;\n81\t\n82\t// Re-export the TP shapes from the public crate root for backwards\n83\t// compatibility with callers that already wrote `crate::TransportParameters`.\n84\tpub use tp::{PreferredAddress, TransportParameter, TransportParameters, VersionNegotiation};\n85\t\n86\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n87\t\n88\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n89\t// Version string and error class.\n90\t//\n91\t// Error values come straight from `#define` and would naturally be\n92\t// untyped in C; in Rust we commit to `u64` because the application\n93\t// API takes them as `uint64_t error_code` parameters and returns\n94\t// them in `get_local_error` etc.\n95\t\n96\t/// Library version string.  C: `VERSION`.\n97\tpub const VERSION: &str = \"1.1.48.0\";\n98\t\n99\t/// Crate-level error type for fallible operations.\n100\t///\n101\t/// Phase 1 stubs return `Result<T, Error>` even though the\n102\t/// `todo!()` bodies don't yet decide which variant to produce —\n103\t/// Phase 3 will fill that in based on the C control flow.  The\n104\t/// initial variant set covers the broad failure shapes; Phase 3\n105\t/// can add more as needed.  Marked `#[non_exhaustive]` so adding\n106\t/// variants later isn't a breaking change.\n107\t#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n108\t#[non_exhaustive]\n109\tpub enum Error {\n110\t    /// Catch-all placeholder for paths Phase 1 has not yet\n111\t    /// distinguished.  Phase 3 should narrow these.\n112\t    Generic,\n113\t    /// Allocation failed.\n114\t    Memory,\n115\t    /// Caller supplied an invalid argument.\n116\t    InvalidArgument,\n117\t    /// A buffer the caller supplied was too small.\n118\t    BufferTooSmall,\n119\t    /// Caller-supplied file does not exist.\n120\t    NoSuchFile,\n121\t    /// Caller-supplied file was malformed.\n122\t    InvalidFile,\n123\t    /// Connection has been disconnected.\n124\t    Disconnected,\n125\t    /// Connection state didn't permit the requested operation.\n126\t    InvalidState,\n127\t    /// Operation produced or received a malformed QUIC frame.\n128\t    InvalidFrame,\n129\t    /// TLS / crypto error.\n130\t    Tls,\n131\t    /// Other QUIC protocol error; the wrapped value is the\n132\t    /// numeric `ERROR_*` code defined in this module.\n133\t    Protocol(u64),\n134\t}\n135\t\n136\timpl core::fmt::Display for Error {\n137\t    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut core::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> core::fmt::Result {\n138\t        write!(f, \"{:?}\", self)\n139\t    }\n140\t}\n141\t\n142\timpl core::error::Error for Error {}\n143\t\n144\t/// Crate-wide `Result` alias defaulting the error type to [`Error`].\n145\tpub type Result<T, E = Error> = core::result::Result<T, E>;\n146\t\n147\t/// Wall-clock instant in microseconds since some application-defined\n148\t/// epoch.  Replaces the C `uint64_t current_time` parameter that\n149\t/// threads through every API call needing \"now\".  The type is\n150\t/// `Copy`, supports arithmetic with [`Duration`], and converts to\n151\t/// `u64` cheaply via `.ticks()` when crossing wire boundaries.\n152\t///\n153\t/// Phase 2 decision: per-call `current_time: Instant` parameters\n154\t/// stay (matches the C source's design — caller decides what time\n155\t/// it is).  No `Clock` trait at the library level; applications\n156\t/// can build a clock-injecting wrapper around the calls if they\n157\t/// want that style.\n158\tpub type Instant = fugit::Instant<u64, 1, 1_000_000>;\n159\t\n160\t/// Duration in microseconds.  Companion to [`Instant`].  Use for\n161\t/// timeouts, RTTs, jitter, etc.  In the C source these are bare\n162\t/// `uint64_t` values labelled by context (`idle_timeout`,\n163\t/// `microsec_latency`, …); the typed alias makes the unit explicit.\n164\tpub type Duration = fugit::Duration<u64, 1, 1_000_000>;\n165\t\n166\t// Error codes (picoquic-internal `ERROR_*` and protocol-defined\n167\t// `TRANSPORT_*` / TLS-alert) live as enums in [`crate::errors`].\n168\tpub use errors::{InternalError, TransportError};\n169\t\n170\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n171\t// Packet sizes and miscellaneous constants.\n172\t\n173\tpub const MAX_PACKET_SIZE: usize = 1536;\n174\tpub const INITIAL_MTU_IPV4: usize = 1252;\n175\tpub const INITIAL_MTU_IPV6: usize = 1232;\n176\tpub const RESET_SECRET_SIZE: usize = 16;\n177\tpub const RESET_PACKET_PAD_SIZE: usize = 23;\n178\tpub const RESET_PACKET_MIN_SIZE: usize = RESET_PACKET_PAD_SIZE + RESET_SECRET_SIZE;\n179\tpub const MAX_CRYPTO_BUFFER_GAP: usize = 16384;\n180\t\n181\tpub const LOG_PACKET_MAX_SEQUENCE: u32 = 100;\n182\t\n183\t/// First 4 bytes of `SHA256(\"QUIC Masque\")`.  Used as a sentinel\n184\t/// interface index where no real OS interface is meaningful.\n185\tpub const RESERVED_IF_INDEX: u32 = 0x09cb8ed3;\n186\t\n187\t// TLS cipher-suite identifiers (IANA).\n188\tpub const AES_128_GCM_SHA256: u16 = 0x1301;\n189\tpub const AES_256_GCM_SHA384: u16 = 0x1302;\n190\tpub const CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256: u16 = 0x1303;\n191\t\n192\tpub const GROUP_SECP256R1: u16 = 23;\n193\t\n194\t// ECN code points for the IP header `tos` byte.\n195\tpub const ECN_ECT_0: u8 = 0x02;\n196\tpub const ECN_ECT_1: u8 = 0x01;\n197\tpub const ECN_CE: u8 = 0x03;\n198\t\n199\t/// C macro `FOURCC(a, b, c, d)`.  Produces a 32-bit code from four\n200\t/// bytes in little-endian order.\n201\tpub const fn fourcc(a: u8, b: u8, c: u8, d: u8) -> u32 {\n202\t    u32::from_le_bytes([a, b, c, d])\n203\t}\n204\t\n205\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n206\t// Connection state.\n207\t\n208\t/// Connection-state machine, listing the QUIC connection states a\n209\t/// `Connection` walks through from initial handshake to teardown.\n210\t/// Discriminants follow the declaration order of the C `state_enum`.\n211\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)]\n212\tpub enum State {\n213\t    ClientInit,\n214\t    ClientInitSent,\n215\t    ClientRenegotiate,\n216\t    ClientRetryReceived,\n217\t    ClientInitResent,\n218\t    ServerInit,\n219\t    ServerHandshake,\n220\t    ClientHandshakeStart,\n221\t    HandshakeFailure,\n222\t    HandshakeFailureResend,\n223\t    ClientAlmostReady,\n224\t    ServerFalseStart,\n225\t    ServerAlmostReady,\n226\t    ClientReadyStart,\n227\t    Ready,\n228\t    Disconnecting,\n229\t    ClosingReceived,\n230\t    Closing,\n231\t    Draining,\n232\t    Disconnected,\n233\t}\n234\t\n235\t// `TransportParameter`, `PreferredAddress`, `VersionNegotiation`,\n236\t// `TransportParameters`, and `TransportParameter0RttKind` live in\n237\t// [`crate::tp`].\n238\t\n239\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n240\t// Packet contexts and enumerated policy types.\n241\t\n242\t/// Encryption-level packet context.  Each value selects one of the\n243\t/// three QUIC packet-number spaces (Initial, Handshake, 1-RTT).\n244\t/// The C enum's trailing `nb_packet_context` count is exposed as\n245\t/// the [`NB_PACKET_CONTEXT`] constant below.\n246\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n247\t#[repr(u8)]\n248\tpub enum PacketContext {\n249\t    Application = 0,\n250\t    Handshake = 1,\n251\t    Initial = 2,\n252\t}\n253\t\n254\t/// Number of packet contexts (matches the variant count of\n255\t/// [`PacketContext`]).\n256\tpub const NB_PACKET_CONTEXT: usize = 3;\n257\t\n258\t/// Path-MTU-discovery policy for a connection.\n259\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n260\t#[repr(u8)]\n261\tpub enum PmtudPolicy {\n262\t    /// Default opportunistic PMTUD.\n263\t    #[default]\n264\t    Basic = 0,\n265\t    /// Force PMTUD as soon as possible.\n266\t    Required = 1,\n267\t    /// Only do PMTUD if a lot of data has to be sent.\n268\t    Delayed = 2,\n269\t    /// Never do PMTUD.\n270\t    Blocked = 3,\n271\t}\n272\t\n273\t/// Spin-bit variant policy.\n274\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n275\t#[repr(u8)]\n276\tpub enum SpinbitVersion {\n277\t    /// Default behaviour, per the spin-bit draft.\n278\t    #[default]\n279\t    Basic = 0,\n280\t    /// Randomise per packet.\n281\t    Random = 1,\n282\t    /// Null behaviour, randomised per path.\n283\t    Null = 2,\n284\t    /// Test-only \"always on\" mode.  Not valid as a per-connection\n285\t    /// override (server only; see [`Quic::set_default_spinbit_policy`]).\n286\t    On = 3,\n287\t}\n288\t\n289\t/// Loss-bit support level.\n290\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n291\t#[repr(u8)]\n292\tpub enum LossbitVersion {\n293\t    /// Loss bits disabled.\n294\t    #[default]\n295\t    None = 0,\n296\t    /// This endpoint sets the loss bits but does not interpret peer's.\n297\t    SendOnly = 1,\n298\t    /// This endpoint both sets and receives loss bits.\n299\t    SendReceive = 2,\n300\t}\n301\t\n302\t/// Path scheduling status — whether a path participates in normal\n303\t/// scheduling or is held in reserve.\n304\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n305\t#[repr(u8)]\n306\tpub enum PathStatus {\n307\t    /// Path is available for sending.\n308\t    #[default]\n309\t    Available = 0,\n310\t    /// Path is held as a backup; data only flows when no Available\n311\t    /// path remains.\n312\t    Backup = 1,\n313\t}\n314\t\n315\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n316\t// Connection ID.\n317\t\n318\tpub const CONNECTION_ID_MIN_SIZE: usize = 0;\n319\tpub const CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE: usize = 20;\n320\t\n321\t/// Fixed-capacity QUIC connection ID.  C: `ConnectionId`.\n322\t///\n323\t/// Stored as a 20-byte buffer plus a length so the type is `Copy`,\n324\t/// matching the C usage where connection IDs are passed by value\n325\t/// in many APIs (`get_local_connection_id`, `create_connection`,\n326\t/// …).  Fields are private — construct via [`Self::clone_from_slice`]\n327\t/// or [`Self::with_size`] and read via [`Self::as_bytes`].\n328\t///\n329\t/// `Ord` is derived as lexicographic over the live bytes (matching\n330\t/// the C `compare_connection_id` shape: per-byte compare under\n331\t/// `min(len_a, len_b)`, then length tie-break).\n332\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash, Default)]\n333\tpub struct ConnectionId {\n334\t    id: [u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE],\n335\t    id_len: u8,\n336\t}\n337\t\n338\timpl ConnectionId {\n339\t    /// Construct a connection id by copying `bytes`.  Returns `None`\n340\t    /// when `bytes.len()` exceeds [`CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE`].\n341\t    pub fn clone_from_slice(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<Self> {\n342\t        if bytes.len() > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE {\n343\t            return None;\n344\t        }\n345\t        let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n346\t        id[..bytes.len()].copy_from_slice(bytes);\n347\t        Some(Self {\n348\t            id,\n349\t            id_len: bytes.len() as u8,\n350\t        })\n351\t    }\n352\t\n353\t    /// Construct a zero-filled connection id of length `len`.\n354\t    /// Returns `None` when `len` exceeds [`CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE`].\n355\t    pub fn with_size(len: usize) -> Option<Self> {\n356\t        if len > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE {\n357\t            return None;\n358\t        }\n359\t        Some(Self {\n360\t            id: [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE],\n361\t            id_len: len as u8,\n362\t        })\n363\t    }\n364\t\n365\t    /// Borrow the live id bytes (`bytes[..len]`).\n366\t    pub fn as_bytes(&self) -> &[u8] {\n367\t        &self.id[..self.id_len as usize]\n368\t    }\n369\t\n370\t    /// Mutable borrow of the live id bytes.\n371\t    pub fn as_bytes_mut(&mut self) -> &mut [u8] {\n372\t        &mut self.id[..self.id_len as usize]\n373\t    }\n374\t\n375\t    /// Number of bytes in the id.\n376\t    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {\n377\t        self.id_len as usize\n378\t    }\n379\t\n380\t    /// `true` when the id has zero length.  Mirrors the C\n381\t    /// `is_connection_id_null` sentinel check.\n382\t    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {\n383\t        self.id_len == 0\n384\t    }\n385\t\n386\t    /// Hash with a 16-byte seed.  C: `connection_id_hash`.\n387\t    pub fn hash_with_seed(&self, seed: &[u8; 16]) -> u64 {\n388\t        crate::siphash::siphash(seed, self.as_bytes())\n389\t    }\n390\t\n391\t    /// Fold the first up-to-8 bytes of the id into a `u64`.\n392\t    /// C: `val64_connection_id`.\n393\t    pub fn val64(&self) -> u64 {\n394\t        let bytes = self.as_bytes();\n395\t        let len = bytes.len().min(8);\n396\t        let mut buf = [0u8; 8];\n397\t        buf[..len].copy_from_slice(&bytes[..len]);\n398\t        u64::from_be_bytes(buf)\n399\t    }\n400\t}\n401\t\n402\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n403\t// IO vectors.\n404\t//\n405\t// The C `picoquic_iovec_t` (and the picotls-side `PtlsIovec*` that\n406\t// aliased it) are gone — every place that used to take an iovec\n407\t// pair now takes a `&[u8]` (single buffer) or `&[&[u8]]` /\n408\t// `Vec<Vec<u8>>` (list of buffers), per the project's\n409\t// \"use Rust's slice/vec types\" policy.\n410\t\n411\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n412\t// Opaque types (forward declarations).\n413\t//\n414\t// `Quic`, `Connection`, and `Path` are\n415\t// defined in `internal.h`.  Phase 1 declares them as empty\n416\t// structs so callers can refer to them; the real layout lands when\n417\t// the internal header is translated.  The `_opaque` field prevents\n418\t// callers from constructing one accidentally, and the `Debug` impl\n419\t// gives the structs a printable shape for log lines.\n420\t\n421\t// Full bodies live in `crate::internal`; pull them in for use within\n422\t// this module's signatures (no re-export — callers reach them as\n423\t// `crate::internal::*`).\n424\tuse crate::internal::{Connection, Path, Quic};\n425\t\n426\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n427\t// Application callback events.\n428\t\n429\t/// Event type passed to the application's stream / connection\n430\t/// callback (see [`StreamDataCallback`]).  Identifies which sort of\n431\t/// notification the stack is delivering — stream data, lifecycle\n432\t/// transition, datagram event, path event, etc.\n433\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n434\tpub enum CallbackEvent {\n435\t    StreamData = 0,\n436\t    StreamFin,\n437\t    StreamReset,\n438\t    StopSending,\n439\t    StatelessReset,\n440\t    Close,\n441\t    ApplicationClose,\n442\t    StreamGap,\n443\t    PrepareToSend,\n444\t    AlmostReady,\n445\t    Ready,\n446\t    Datagram,\n447\t    VersionNegotiation,\n448\t    RequestAlpnList,\n449\t    SetAlpn,\n450\t    PacingChanged,\n451\t    PrepareDatagram,\n452\t    DatagramAcked,\n453\t    DatagramLost,\n454\t    DatagramSpurious,\n455\t    PathAvailable,\n456\t    PathSuspended,\n457\t    PathDeleted,\n458\t    PathQualityChanged,\n459\t    PathAddressObserved,\n460\t    AppWakeup,\n461\t    NextPathAllowed,\n462\t}\n463\t\n464\t// Transport-parameter shapes live in [`crate::tp`].\n465\t\n466\t// Stream-id decomposition lives on [`crate::stream::StreamId`].\n467\t\n468\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n469\t// Time management.\n470\t\n471\t/// Wall-clock microseconds since the Unix epoch.\n472\t///\n473\t/// The C body reads the OS clock; in Rust this requires the `std`\n474\t/// feature (`std::time::SystemTime`).  The `cfg`-gated split lands\n475\t/// when the crate gains its `std` feature and the rest of the\n476\t/// `no_std + alloc` plumbing — for Phase 1 the function is just a\n477\t/// `todo!()` stub.\n478\tpub fn current_time() -> u64 {\n479\t    use std::time::{SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH};\n480\t    SystemTime::now()\n481\t        .duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH)\n482\t        .map(|d| d.as_micros() as u64)\n483\t        .unwrap_or(0)\n484\t}\n485\t\n486\timpl Quic {\n487\t    /// Virtual time used by this QUIC context (wall-clock or\n488\t    /// simulated, depending on whether a simulated-time pointer was\n489\t    /// supplied at creation).  C: `get_quic_time`.\n490\t    pub fn time(&self) -> u64 {\n491\t        current_time()\n492\t    }\n493\t}\n494\t\n495\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n496\t// Application callbacks (function-pointer typedefs → traits).\n497\t\n498\t/// Stream/event callback installed on a QUIC context or connection.\n499\t/// Folds the C `void* callback_ctx` into the trait implementor's\n500\t/// state; `stream_ctx` is per-stream and remains a raw pointer"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6e30a456-15c0-4e3c-af27-9f4dab375915","uuid":"75b47b2c-a75e-48d4-aa28-602951563696","timestamp":"2026-05-05T21:18:41.361Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/lib.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/quic.h`.\n//!\n//! `quic.h` is the kitchen-sink public header for the\n//! quic-core library: it defines the protocol error codes, the\n//! transport-parameter identifiers, the application-facing enums, the\n//! opaque QUIC / connection / path types, the public structs that\n//! cross the API boundary (`TransportParameters`, `PathQuality`,\n//! `PerAckState`, `CongestionAlgorithm`, …)\n//! and the dozens of free functions that make up the application API.\n//!\n//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n//!\n//! Pointer-shape and translation policy notes that apply throughout\n//! this module:\n//!\n//! * `Quic`, `Connection`, `Path` are\n//!   opaque types defined in `internal.h`.  Phase 1 declares\n//!   them as empty structs here so the public API can refer to them;\n//!   the real layout lands when the internal header is translated.\n//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields map to\n//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`].  The C source uses tagged unions of\n//!   `sockaddr_in` / `sockaddr_in6`, which `SocketAddr` models\n//!   directly.  Output parameters of type `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n//!   become a `SocketAddr` returned by value.\n//! * Function-pointer typedefs become traits (one trait per typedef\n//!   by default).  Trait grouping is used for the four-function\n//!   `CongestionAlgorithm` vtable, where the C side\n//!   always installs the four together.\n//! * `void*` \"callback context\" arguments are folded into the trait\n//!   implementor's state.  Per-stream / per-path application\n//!   contexts that flow through the stack (e.g. `stream_ctx`,\n//!   `app_stream_ctx`) become `Option<Box<dyn core::any::Any>>` —\n//!   the application produces them, downcasts on retrieval.\n//! * C `int` / `int64_t` return values that encode 0/-1 or 0/error\n//!   status become `Result<T, Error>` — the crate-level `Error` enum\n//!   doesn't exist yet, so `()` is a placeholder per the Phase 1\n//!   contract.  `i32` / `i64` are kept where the C value is a real\n//!   integer (e.g., wake delays in microseconds, interface index).\n//! * Single-bit `unsigned int : 1` bitfields collapse to `bool`.\n//! * `va_list` has no Rust counterpart; the `_v` log helpers are\n//!   omitted in favour of one entry point that takes\n//!   `core::fmt::Arguments<'_>`.\n//! * `extern` global state (`congestion_control_algorithms`)\n//!   becomes accessor functions that hand out a borrowed slice; the\n//!   companion `_nb_` length variable disappears (the slice carries\n//!   its length).\n\n// Many translated functions mirror C signatures with >7 parameters.\n// Builder patterns or shape changes are out of scope for Phase 1.\n#![allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]\n\npub mod arena;\npub mod binlog;\npub mod bytestream;\npub mod cc_common;\npub mod config;\npub mod crypto;\npub mod errors;\npub mod frames;\npub mod hash;\npub mod header_protection;\npub mod internal;\npub mod lb;\npub mod logger;\npub mod packet_loop;\npub mod performance_log;\npub mod qlog;\npub mod siphash;\npub mod socks;\npub mod socks_socket2;\npub mod splay;\npub mod stream;\npub mod sys;\n#[cfg(test)]\npub mod tests;\npub mod textlog;\npub mod tls;\npub mod tls_api;\npub mod tp;\npub mod utils;\n\n// Re-export the TP shapes from the public crate root for backwards\n// compatibility with callers that already wrote `crate::TransportParameters`.\npub use tp::{PreferredAddress, TransportParameter, TransportParameters, VersionNegotiation};\n\nuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Version string and error class.\n//\n// Error values come straight from `#define` and would naturally be\n// untyped in C; in Rust we commit to `u64` because the application\n// API takes them as `uint64_t error_code` parameters and returns\n// them in `get_local_error` etc.\n\n/// Library version string.  C: `VERSION`.\npub const VERSION: &str = \"1.1.48.0\";\n\n/// Crate-level error type for fallible operations.\n///\n/// Phase 1 stubs return `Result<T, Error>` even though the\n/// `todo!()` bodies don't yet decide which variant to produce —\n/// Phase 3 will fill that in based on the C control flow.  The\n/// initial variant set covers the broad failure shapes; Phase 3\n/// can add more as needed.  Marked `#[non_exhaustive]` so adding\n/// variants later isn't a breaking change.\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub enum Error {\n    /// Catch-all placeholder for paths Phase 1 has not yet\n    /// distinguished.  Phase 3 should narrow these.\n    Generic,\n    /// Allocation failed.\n    Memory,\n    /// Caller supplied an invalid argument.\n    InvalidArgument,\n    /// A buffer the caller supplied was too small.\n    BufferTooSmall,\n    /// Caller-supplied file does not exist.\n    NoSuchFile,\n    /// Caller-supplied file was malformed.\n    InvalidFile,\n    /// Connection has been disconnected.\n    Disconnected,\n    /// Connection state didn't permit the requested operation.\n    InvalidState,\n    /// Operation produced or received a malformed QUIC frame.\n    InvalidFrame,\n    /// TLS / crypto error.\n    Tls,\n    /// Other QUIC protocol error; the wrapped value is the\n    /// numeric `ERROR_*` code defined in this module.\n    Protocol(u64),\n}\n\nimpl core::fmt::Display for Error {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut core::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> core::fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"{:?}\", self)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl core::error::Error for Error {}\n\n/// Crate-wide `Result` alias defaulting the error type to [`Error`].\npub type Result<T, E = Error> = core::result::Result<T, E>;\n\n/// Wall-clock instant in microseconds since some application-defined\n/// epoch.  Replaces the C `uint64_t current_time` parameter that\n/// threads through every API call needing \"now\".  The type is\n/// `Copy`, supports arithmetic with [`Duration`], and converts to\n/// `u64` cheaply via `.ticks()` when crossing wire boundaries.\n///\n/// Phase 2 decision: per-call `current_time: Instant` parameters\n/// stay (matches the C source's design — caller decides what time\n/// it is).  No `Clock` trait at the library level; applications\n/// can build a clock-injecting wrapper around the calls if they\n/// want that style.\npub type Instant = fugit::Instant<u64, 1, 1_000_000>;\n\n/// Duration in microseconds.  Companion to [`Instant`].  Use for\n/// timeouts, RTTs, jitter, etc.  In the C source these are bare\n/// `uint64_t` values labelled by context (`idle_timeout`,\n/// `microsec_latency`, …); the typed alias makes the unit explicit.\npub type Duration = fugit::Duration<u64, 1, 1_000_000>;\n\n// Error codes (picoquic-internal `ERROR_*` and protocol-defined\n// `TRANSPORT_*` / TLS-alert) live as enums in [`crate::errors`].\npub use errors::{InternalError, TransportError};\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Packet sizes and miscellaneous constants.\n\npub const MAX_PACKET_SIZE: usize = 1536;\npub const INITIAL_MTU_IPV4: usize = 1252;\npub const INITIAL_MTU_IPV6: usize = 1232;\npub const RESET_SECRET_SIZE: usize = 16;\npub const RESET_PACKET_PAD_SIZE: usize = 23;\npub const RESET_PACKET_MIN_SIZE: usize = RESET_PACKET_PAD_SIZE + RESET_SECRET_SIZE;\npub const MAX_CRYPTO_BUFFER_GAP: usize = 16384;\n\npub const LOG_PACKET_MAX_SEQUENCE: u32 = 100;\n\n/// First 4 bytes of `SHA256(\"QUIC Masque\")`.  Used as a sentinel\n/// interface index where no real OS interface is meaningful.\npub const RESERVED_IF_INDEX: u32 = 0x09cb8ed3;\n\n// TLS cipher-suite identifiers (IANA).\npub const AES_128_GCM_SHA256: u16 = 0x1301;\npub const AES_256_GCM_SHA384: u16 = 0x1302;\npub const CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256: u16 = 0x1303;\n\npub const GROUP_SECP256R1: u16 = 23;\n\n// ECN code points for the IP header `tos` byte.\npub const ECN_ECT_0: u8 = 0x02;\npub const ECN_ECT_1: u8 = 0x01;\npub const ECN_CE: u8 = 0x03;\n\n/// C macro `FOURCC(a, b, c, d)`.  Produces a 32-bit code from four\n/// bytes in little-endian order.\npub const fn fourcc(a: u8, b: u8, c: u8, d: u8) -> u32 {\n    u32::from_le_bytes([a, b, c, d])\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Connection state.\n\n/// Connection-state machine, listing the QUIC connection states a\n/// `Connection` walks through from initial handshake to teardown.\n/// Discriminants follow the declaration order of the C `state_enum`.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord)]\npub enum State {\n    ClientInit,\n    ClientInitSent,\n    ClientRenegotiate,\n    ClientRetryReceived,\n    ClientInitResent,\n    ServerInit,\n    ServerHandshake,\n    ClientHandshakeStart,\n    HandshakeFailure,\n    HandshakeFailureResend,\n    ClientAlmostReady,\n    ServerFalseStart,\n    ServerAlmostReady,\n    ClientReadyStart,\n    Ready,\n    Disconnecting,\n    ClosingReceived,\n    Closing,\n    Draining,\n    Disconnected,\n}\n\n// `TransportParameter`, `PreferredAddress`, `VersionNegotiation`,\n// `TransportParameters`, and `TransportParameter0RttKind` live in\n// [`crate::tp`].\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Packet contexts and enumerated policy types.\n\n/// Encryption-level packet context.  Each value selects one of the\n/// three QUIC packet-number spaces (Initial, Handshake, 1-RTT).\n/// The C enum's trailing `nb_packet_context` count is exposed as\n/// the [`NB_PACKET_CONTEXT`] constant below.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n#[repr(u8)]\npub enum PacketContext {\n    Application = 0,\n    Handshake = 1,\n    Initial = 2,\n}\n\n/// Number of packet contexts (matches the variant count of\n/// [`PacketContext`]).\npub const NB_PACKET_CONTEXT: usize = 3;\n\n/// Path-MTU-discovery policy for a connection.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n#[repr(u8)]\npub enum PmtudPolicy {\n    /// Default opportunistic PMTUD.\n    #[default]\n    Basic = 0,\n    /// Force PMTUD as soon as possible.\n    Required = 1,\n    /// Only do PMTUD if a lot of data has to be sent.\n    Delayed = 2,\n    /// Never do PMTUD.\n    Blocked = 3,\n}\n\n/// Spin-bit variant policy.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n#[repr(u8)]\npub enum SpinbitVersion {\n    /// Default behaviour, per the spin-bit draft.\n    #[default]\n    Basic = 0,\n    /// Randomise per packet.\n    Random = 1,\n    /// Null behaviour, randomised per path.\n    Null = 2,\n    /// Test-only \"always on\" mode.  Not valid as a per-connection\n    /// override (server only; see [`Quic::set_default_spinbit_policy`]).\n    On = 3,\n}\n\n/// Loss-bit support level.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n#[repr(u8)]\npub enum LossbitVersion {\n    /// Loss bits disabled.\n    #[default]\n    None = 0,\n    /// This endpoint sets the loss bits but does not interpret peer's.\n    SendOnly = 1,\n    /// This endpoint both sets and receives loss bits.\n    SendReceive = 2,\n}\n\n/// Path scheduling status — whether a path participates in normal\n/// scheduling or is held in reserve.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Default)]\n#[repr(u8)]\npub enum PathStatus {\n    /// Path is available for sending.\n    #[default]\n    Available = 0,\n    /// Path is held as a backup; data only flows when no Available\n    /// path remains.\n    Backup = 1,\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Connection ID.\n\npub const CONNECTION_ID_MIN_SIZE: usize = 0;\npub const CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE: usize = 20;\n\n/// Fixed-capacity QUIC connection ID.  C: `ConnectionId`.\n///\n/// Stored as a 20-byte buffer plus a length so the type is `Copy`,\n/// matching the C usage where connection IDs are passed by value\n/// in many APIs (`get_local_connection_id`, `create_connection`,\n/// …).  Fields are private — construct via [`Self::clone_from_slice`]\n/// or [`Self::with_size`] and read via [`Self::as_bytes`].\n///\n/// `Ord` is derived as lexicographic over the live bytes (matching\n/// the C `compare_connection_id` shape: per-byte compare under\n/// `min(len_a, len_b)`, then length tie-break).\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, PartialOrd, Ord, Hash, Default)]\npub struct ConnectionId {\n    id: [u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE],\n    id_len: u8,\n}\n\nimpl ConnectionId {\n    /// Construct a connection id by copying `bytes`.  Returns `None`\n    /// when `bytes.len()` exceeds [`CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE`].\n    pub fn clone_from_slice(bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<Self> {\n        if bytes.len() > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let mut id = [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE];\n        id[..bytes.len()].copy_from_slice(bytes);\n        Some(Self {\n            id,\n            id_len: bytes.len() as u8,\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// Construct a zero-filled connection id of length `len`.\n    /// Returns `None` when `len` exceeds [`CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE`].\n    pub fn with_size(len: usize) -> Option<Self> {\n        if len > CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE {\n            return None;\n        }\n        Some(Self {\n            id: [0u8; CONNECTION_ID_MAX_SIZE],\n            id_len: len as u8,\n        })\n    }\n\n    /// Borrow the live id bytes (`bytes[..len]`).\n    pub fn as_bytes(&self) -> &[u8] {\n        &self.id[..self.id_len as usize]\n    }\n\n    /// Mutable borrow of the live id bytes.\n    pub fn as_bytes_mut(&mut self) -> &mut [u8] {\n        &mut self.id[..self.id_len as usize]\n    }\n\n    /// Number of bytes in the id.\n    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {\n        self.id_len as usize\n    }\n\n    /// `true` when the id has zero length.  Mirrors the C\n    /// `is_connection_id_null` sentinel check.\n    pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {\n        self.id_len == 0\n    }\n\n    /// Hash with a 16-byte seed.  C: `connection_id_hash`.\n    pub fn hash_with_seed(&self, seed: &[u8; 16]) -> u64 {\n        crate::siphash::siphash(seed, self.as_bytes())\n    }\n\n    /// Fold the first up-to-8 bytes of the id into a `u64`.\n    /// C: `val64_connection_id`.\n    pub fn val64(&self) -> u64 {\n        let bytes = self.as_bytes();\n        let len = bytes.len().min(8);\n        let mut buf = [0u8; 8];\n        buf[..len].copy_from_slice(&bytes[..len]);\n        u64::from_be_bytes(buf)\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// IO vectors.\n//\n// The C `picoquic_iovec_t` (and the picotls-side `PtlsIovec*` that\n// aliased it) are gone — every place that used to take an iovec\n// pair now takes a `&[u8]` (single buffer) or `&[&[u8]]` /\n// `Vec<Vec<u8>>` (list of buffers), per the project's\n// \"use Rust's slice/vec types\" policy.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Opaque types (forward declarations).\n//\n// `Quic`, `Connection`, and `Path` are\n// defined in `internal.h`.  Phase 1 declares them as empty\n// structs so callers can refer to them; the real layout lands when\n// the internal header is translated.  The `_opaque` field prevents\n// callers from constructing one accidentally, and the `Debug` impl\n// gives the structs a printable shape for log lines.\n\n// Full bodies live in `crate::internal`; pull them in for use within\n// this module's signatures (no re-export — callers reach them as\n// `crate::internal::*`).\nuse crate::internal::{Connection, Path, Quic};\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Application callback events.\n\n/// Event type passed to the application's stream / connection\n/// callback (see [`StreamDataCallback`]).  Identifies which sort of\n/// notification the stack is delivering — stream data, lifecycle\n/// transition, datagram event, path event, etc.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub enum CallbackEvent {\n    StreamData = 0,\n    StreamFin,\n    StreamReset,\n    StopSending,\n    StatelessReset,\n    Close,\n    ApplicationClose,\n    StreamGap,\n    PrepareToSend,\n    AlmostReady,\n    Ready,\n    Datagram,\n    VersionNegotiation,\n    RequestAlpnList,\n    SetAlpn,\n    PacingChanged,\n    PrepareDatagram,\n    DatagramAcked,\n    DatagramLost,\n    DatagramSpurious,\n    PathAvailable,\n    PathSuspended,\n    PathDeleted,\n    PathQualityChanged,\n    PathAddressObserved,\n    AppWakeup,\n    NextPathAllowed,\n}\n\n// Transport-parameter shapes live in [`crate::tp`].\n\n// Stream-id decomposition lives on [`crate::stream::StreamId`].\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Time management.\n\n/// Wall-clock microseconds since the Unix epoch.\n///\n/// The C body reads the OS clock; in Rust this requires the `std`\n/// feature (`std::time::SystemTime`).  The `cfg`-gated split lands\n/// when the crate gains its `std` feature and the rest of the\n/// `no_std + alloc` plumbing — for Phase 1 the function is just a\n/// `todo!()` stub.\npub fn current_time() -> u64 {\n    use std::time::{SystemTime, UNIX_EPOCH};\n    SystemTime::now()\n        .duration_since(UNIX_EPOCH)\n        .map(|d| d.as_micros() as u64)\n        .unwrap_or(0)\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Virtual time used by this QUIC context (wall-clock or\n    /// simulated, depending on whether a simulated-time pointer was\n    /// supplied at creation).  C: `get_quic_time`.\n    pub fn time(&self) -> u64 {\n        current_time()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Application callbacks (function-pointer typedefs → traits).\n\n/// Stream/event callback installed on a QUIC context or connection.\n/// Folds the C `void* callback_ctx` into the trait implementor's\n/// state; `stream_ctx` is per-stream and remains a raw pointer","numLines":500,"startLine":1,"totalLines":3321}}}
