{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q4ATnhoyGwtrfPM8wPS7HR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! SipHash-2-4 reference implementation, vendored from the upstream\n2\t//! public-domain release.  The C source exposes a single `siphash`\n3\t//! function whose `outlen` parameter selects between the 64-bit and\n4\t//! 128-bit digest variants; only the 64-bit variant has any in-tree\n5\t//! caller, so the Rust API drops the 128-bit form entirely and\n6\t//! returns the 64-bit digest as a `u64` (every call site immediately\n7\t//! converted the byte digest with `from_le_bytes`).\n8\t//!\n9\t//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only; Phase 4 fills the body.\n10\t//!\n11\t//! Phase 4 should consider replacing this hand-rolled implementation\n12\t//! with the `siphasher` crate, which exposes the same algorithm\n13\t//! behind a `Hasher` interface and is no_std-friendly.\n14\t\n15\t/// Compute the 64-bit SipHash-2-4 digest of `input` under `key`.\n16\t///\n17\t/// The 128-bit secret `key` is taken by reference to a fixed-size\n18\t/// array because the algorithm reads exactly 16 bytes.\n19\t///\n20\t/// C: `siphash(..., outlen = 8)` followed by `from_le_bytes` at every\n21\t/// call site.\n22\tpub fn siphash(input: &[u8], key: &[u8; 16]) -> u64 {\n23\t    fn sip_round(v0: &mut u64, v1: &mut u64, v2: &mut u64, v3: &mut u64) {\n24\t        *v0 = v0.wrapping_add(*v1);\n25\t        *v1 = v1.rotate_left(13);\n26\t        *v1 ^= *v0;\n27\t        *v0 = v0.rotate_left(32);\n28\t        *v2 = v2.wrapping_add(*v3);\n29\t        *v3 = v3.rotate_left(16);\n30\t        *v3 ^= *v2;"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FNJRg8YhsoxF5PXikNyYX1","session_id":"6e30a456-15c0-4e3c-af27-9f4dab375915","uuid":"a2ea65eb-e7ad-4d2b-bb64-d0441d4489bf","timestamp":"2026-05-05T21:21:40.211Z"}
