{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GDreRwjCkCxFnx52gYWQXV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Token-based hash table.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Replaces the C `picohash_table` (an open-addressed table with\n4\t//! chained collision lists, intrusive `picohash_item`s embedded in\n5\t//! the caller's structs and a function-pointer trio for hashing /\n6\t//! comparing / projecting the embedded item).\n7\t//!\n8\t//! ## API shape\n9\t//!\n10\t//! `HashTable<K, V>` is a slotmap-backed hash map.  `K: Hash + Eq`\n11\t//! provides the key contract; `V` is whatever the caller stores\n12\t//! (usually a token into some other arena — see Phase-4 plan\n13\t//! below).  The trio of C function pointers is gone: hashing comes\n14\t//! from `K`'s `Hash` impl, comparison from `Eq`, and the table\n15\t//! never reaches into the caller's struct so no `key_to_item`\n16\t//! callback is needed.\n17\t//!\n18\t//! Operations return [`HashToken`]s — opaque (`idx, generation`)\n19\t//! handles into this table's slot vector.  Tokens are `Copy`,\n20\t//! survive insertion and removal of *other* keys, but become stale\n21\t//! the moment the keyed entry is removed (the slot's generation\n22\t//! bumps).  [`HashTable::get`] returns `None` for stale tokens —\n23\t//! the safe equivalent of the C dangling-`HashItem*` use-after-free.\n24\t//!\n25\t//! ## Phase 4 plan — implementation\n26\t//!\n27\t//! Phase 4: hand-rolled slotmap implementation.  One of:\n28\t//!\n29\t//! 1. **Hand-roll the slotmap.**  Internal layout (illustrative):\n30\t//!\n31\t//!    ```ignore\n32\t//!    struct Slot<K, V> {\n33\t//!        generation: u32,\n34\t//!        state: SlotState<K, V>,\n35\t//!    }\n36\t//!    enum SlotState<K, V> {\n37\t//!        Free   { next_free: Option<u32> },\n38\t//!        Filled { hash: u64, key: K, value: V, next_in_bin: Option<u32> },\n39\t//!    }\n40\t//!    pub struct HashTable<K, V> {\n41\t//!        slots: Vec<Slot<K, V>>,\n42\t//!        free:  Option<u32>,\n43\t//!        bins:  Vec<Option<u32>>,\n44\t//!        seed:  [u8; 16],\n45\t//!        len:   usize,\n46\t//!    }\n47\t//!    ```\n48\t//!\n49\t//!    `next_in_bin` lives inside the slot, not the user struct, so\n50\t//!    no `unsafe`.  Lookup hashes `K` to a bin index, walks the\n51\t//!    chain by following `next_in_bin` slot indices, comparing each\n52\t//!    `slot.key` against the target.  Insert pushes a new slot\n53\t//!    (or pops one off the free list) and threads it onto the bin\n54\t//!    chain.  Remove unlinks, marks the slot `Free`, bumps\n55\t//!    generation, and adds it to the free list.\n56\t//!\n57\t//! 2. **Build on the `slotmap` crate.**  `slotmap::SlotMap` already\n58\t//!    provides the slot/generation/free-list machinery; this module\n59\t//!    layers the bin-array indexing on top.  The crate is\n60\t//!    `no_std`-friendly with the right features and would let us\n61\t//!    delete maybe 80% of the implementation.\n62\t//!\n63\t//! Either implementation should keep `HashToken` byte-compatible\n64\t//! with the public API below — the choice is local.\n65\t//!\n66\t//! ## Phase 4 plan — call sites\n67\t//!\n68\t//! In the C tree, every hash table is intrusive: the value type\n69\t//! embeds a `HashItem` field and the table's bins point straight\n70\t//! into the middle of those user-owned objects.  In the Rust port\n71\t//! the embedding turns into a *membership token*: the parent\n72\t//! struct holds `Option<HashToken>` for each table it can be in,\n73\t//! and the table's slot stores the parent's `ConnectionToken`\n74\t//! (or other token type) into the arena that owns the parent.\n75\t//!\n76\t//! For example, `quic.connection_by_id: HashTable<ConnectionId,\n77\t//! ConnectionToken>` points at connections that live in\n78\t//! `quic.connections: Arena<Connection>`.  A `Connection` carries\n79\t//! `connection_by_id_membership: Option<HashToken>`; on removal the\n80\t//! Connection hands that token back for an O(1) `connection_by_id.remove`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01FNJRg8YhsoxF5PXikNyYX1","session_id":"6e30a456-15c0-4e3c-af27-9f4dab375915","uuid":"7c2b1e8c-fc89-4f68-a12b-f2de7835eae0","timestamp":"2026-05-05T21:31:16.960Z"}
